y heterogeneous  and you can have subclonal  populations of cells we developed  another pipeline that we call the rare  pipeline  and it allows you to detect very low  allele fraction structural variants  with very high sensitivity and very high  ppv so low false positives  and we can go down to as low as one  percent variant allele fraction  so at 300x effective coverage so that  means just a single flow cell  run for a day on our instrument we can  detect  translocations inversions deletions  insertions and duplications down to five  percent allele fraction  keep in mind that it's completely  unbiased there's no amplification  there's no panel  we show you every structural variant  down to five percent of the diffraction  and if you want to run your instrument  longer for two to four days  then we can go down to as little as one  percent allele fraction  so that means that if just two percent  of the cells in your tumor have a  heterozygous translocation  anywhere we will still pick that up  no other technology can do this or comes  even close to that  so i want to show you a couple examples  from cancer keep in mind that  all of these findings really apply to  genetic disease as well  cancer is typically a little harder so  i'll show you those examples  first so the first paper i want to talk  about is from  dr jim brooch who is director of  personalized medicine at the penn state  institute in hershey and  he published work on a dozen leukemia  samples that were analyzed with ngs and  bionano  and in this paper he shows a number of  variants  found in different leukemia samples like  here's a case of a non-hodgkin's  lymphoma where the t lymphocytes were  cultured  as a germline control but when you look  at the lymphoma cells  you actually see that there's a three  megabase inversion  this inversion was missed by ngs was not  seen on a karyotype because it's just  too small for that  but what's important here is that it  cuts right through the gene p10 that's  labeled in red  at the bottom p10 is a known tumor  suppressor gene  and when this gene is disrupted it has a  severe effect on the  development of the tumor and also on the  treatment really because there are  dozens of compounds and trials  now dozens of trials going on for  compounds that rescue  p10 mutations the slip in the other  allele was picked up with sequencing  so here you have this combination where  the bionano optical mapping of genome  imaging was really essential  to find this variant that could  immediately have changed the treatment  for this patient now in the paper they  show  a number of leukemia genes on the left  there that were affected by multiple  variants and multiple samples  that makes a lot of sense but they also  found a lot of other cancer-associated  genes that were not previously  linked to leukemia and on top of that  dozens and dozens  of genes that were not previously linked  to cancer at all  that were affected in multiple samples  so there's an enormous potential of  discovery here  just out of the single simple study on  12 leukemia genomes  and that is really because there simply  was no method to look at gene and white  structural variation  with this type of sensitivity before so  these studies just couldn't be done  there's still an enormous amount of  new genes and new biomarkers to be  discovered  and actually if you pay attention to  this study  they followed up by looking at some of  these genes looking at the tcag  tcga data and finding that they really  do affect  survival in some of these patients  another study that just came out in bio  archives is of great interest to us  this is from a team at the radabout  university namek in the netherlands and  they've always been at the forefront of  bringing new technology into the clinic  and that's what they're doing here by  comparing our technology  our genome imaging technology with  standard cytogenetics  and standard cytogenetics is karyotyping  as we've shown before  there's fish which works well but you  only use specific probes to specific  genes so it has a very limited  field of view in a sense and in  chromosomal microarray  that's only capable of detecting uh  duplications and deletions  uh but cannot find um the exact  structure  and kind of find balanced events so in  the study they compared bionano against  all of these methods combined in 48  samples  and amazingly found 100 concordance  meaning that in  all 48 samples by nano picked up all the  clinically  reported structural variants from  karyotype and  fish and array combined that is just  incredible and many  sequencing based methods have tried to  do this and failed to replace  cytogenetics  we are the first technology that can  actually do that and  um besides these more standard variants  like a bcr-able translocation that they  found  they had a sample with complex  chromothripsis that was resolved  unambiguously  uh by bionano and on top of that they  also  discovered 19 novel gene fusions  never before reported and some of them  have a  translocation partner that is known to  be important like bcr  at the bottom there but with the new  translocation partner that had not been  reported  before so again an enormous amount of  discovery potential in this type of data  and the power of our technology really  is  that we could take a workflow like you  see on the left there the cyto lab  workflow where you have depending on the  input source  and the tests that you're doing an  enormous amount of different procedures  with different equipment  with culturing of cells with different  microscope and array scanners and  and hybridization uh  ovens and all of that and all of that  could be replaced with the workflow on  the right  there's just a simple single instrument  single uh dna extraction workflow  and then a single piece of software that  can give you array data fish data  karyotype data  all of that combined  we have many more exciting cancer  discovery stories  here is a sample a pair of  hepatocellular carcinomas  that were analyzed two liver tumors that  looked fairly identical  under a microscope but if you look on  the left you see a  fairly simple karyotype or a simple  genome with just a few rearrangements  but on the right you see a massive  number of rearrangements  massive changes in copy number and  translocations  and the difference between the two is  really the lack of presence of  replication stress  and we also found the cause of that the  figure above that there you see  that we detected the hbv so the  hepatitis b  virus insertion site right upstream  of a cyclin e1 gene and that cycling  gene  regulates the cell cycle the viral  promoter turns on that cycling gene  makes that cell replicate  much faster than the dna replication can  keep up with  and that causes this genome to really  fall apart so not only do we see  the signature of that replication stress  but we also  see the cause of that that hepatitis  insertion  i want to switch to genetic disease and  finish up with a few more examples here  here is a patient with duchenne muscular  dystrophy  severe degenerative muscular dystrophy  that affects boys  because it's excellent and it's a very  very large gene  the largest gene in the human genome  really with 79 exons  and this patient had all the symptoms of  duchenne's  but was analyzed with microarray with  whole genome sequence  with mlpa to look for the presence or  absence of all of the  exons all the exons were pcr amplified  and sanger sequenced  and yet no variants were found in this  gene  but when it was analyzed by nano we  immediately picked up this five megabase  inversion which you see here  which cos which cuts the dystrophin gene  labeled dmd on the right  are right in half so this disrupted the  gene entirely  uh evidence of this was found uh in the  rna after muscle biopsy  picked up immediately by bioneno they're  showing it twice here with two labeling  enzymes  uh mark ebbert who's also speaking the  speaker at the symposium  uh used our technology to identify a  repeat expansion at the c9 or f72 gene  which is the cause of als or one of the  causes of als  and what you're looking at here is a  number of alleles  a number of maps that were built by our  system  with various sizes of the insertion so  you see that the spacing between these  labels from top to bottom  increases all the way to 32 kb insertion  and we see several molecules supporting  each of these expansions  and this comes from a single brain  biopsy from a patient  who died of als so not only are we able  to span  and measure that repeat expansion that  consists entirely out of g's and c's  you cannot pcr amplified is no modern  technology can  span that by nano can we also see this  in a mosaic pattern so we can tell you  exactly what allele fraction there is  for each of these expansions in the  brain  now there's another muscular dystrophy  um where by nano plays an important part  and that is fshd  that stands for fascio scapular humeral  muscular dystrophy  it's a muscle dystrophy that affects  mostly the shoulders and the arms  and the face and the only way to  diagnose this  method is with saturn blood using  radioactive labeling  a lot of universities and centers have  to get rid of this radioactive labeling  or want to  and the reason why this only works with  saturn blot is because there is a very  large repeat  septal numeric of chromosome 4 and one  that contracts below 10 copies  then it causes the disease by changing  the chromatin structure  now to genotype this you have to exactly  measure this large repeat  and you have to genotype the presence of  the pathogenic or non-pathogenic allele  all of this is very complex but bionano  does this very beautifully as you see  there  the magenta part represents the repeat  and you see  a contracted repeat on top in a patient  sample  and then a wild type repeat in a control  sample below that  and our enfocus software generates a  summary report with a genotype  that then can be used by labs to develop  a clinical test  and that's exactly what perkin element  genomics and university of iowa have  already done  university of iowa is the largest center  in north america for fshd testing and  they are  using our technology for that  now if you want to get by nanodata  we made it easier than ever for you to  access that  starting from the right you can simply  purchase a system starts at 150  000 and then the cost per genome is just  four hundred and fifty dollars  we also have reagent rental models where  you commit to 120 genomes in six months  and we give you an instrument for free  to use  or you can use our full service offering  where you simply send our samples  send your samples to buy nano and we  give you the data back  and that starts at 650  i would highly recommend you have a look  at our website we have a library of  videos  from presentations from other scientists  who have used our technology and  made some spectacular findings with that  and with that i want to thank you for  your time  you're welcome to ask any questions here  and we will get back to you  as quickly as possible with them so  thank you
 

